      "completion_tokens": 1248,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical substance with the chemical formula **$H_2O$**. This means that each molecule of water is made up of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what makes water unique and important:\n\n### 1. Chemical Composition\n*   **Oxygen:** This is a highly reactive gas that we need to breathe. In water, it is chemically stable.\n*   **Hydrogen:** This is the lightest and most abundant element in the universe. It is highly flammable.\n*   **The Bond:** When these two elements combine, they form a covalent bond. The result is a molecule that is neither flammable nor explosive, but is essential for life.\n\n### 2. Molecular Structure\nWater molecules are not straight lines; they are bent. The angle between the two hydrogen atoms and the oxygen atom is about 104.5 degrees. This bent shape makes water a **polar molecule**.\n*   This polarity means one end of the molecule has a slight positive charge, and the other end has a slight negative charge.\n*   This allows water to stick to itself (cohesion) and to other substances (adhesion), and it makes water the \"universal solvent,\" meaning it can dissolve more substances than any other liquid.\n\n### 3. States of Matter\nWater is one of the few substances on Earth that commonly exists naturally in all three states of matter:\n*   **Solid (Ice):** When water freezes, the molecules slow down and lock into a rigid, crystalline structure. This is why ice floats on liquid water (unlike most other substances).\n*   **Liquid:** This is the state we use for drinking, washing, and swimming. It is the only state that can cover the Earth's surface in large amounts.\n*   **Gas (Steam/Vapor):** When water is heated, it turns into a gas that is invisible to the naked eye.\n\n### 4. Why It Is Essential for Life\nWater is the foundation of life as we know it:\n*   **Biological Medium:** It makes up about 60% of the human body and is the medium in which biological reactions occur.\n*   **Temperature Regulation:** Water has a very high \"specific heat,\" meaning it takes a lot of energy to heat it up and a lot of energy to cool it down. This helps regulate the temperature of living organisms and the planet.\n*   **Transport:** Because it is a good solvent, water acts as a delivery system in the body, carrying nutrients to cells and removing waste."
